Context: this is the third draft of 'Analysis situs' (June 1986). It belongs to Grothendieck's attempt to axiomatize 'tame' (moderate) topology, the programme of Esquisse §5-6, by purely order-theoretic and 'algebraic' data. The basic object, called a 'chiffon' on neighbouring pages, is a family F of 'objects' (think of closed, compact or stratified pieces) with two relations. The first is an inclusion order ≤. The second, ≪, is a 'strong inclusion' relation (shadow/interior relation; the notes use Omb(X) for the 'ombre' (shadow) of X). Page 50 takes stock after the failed attempts of p. 49, where he could not prove two lemmas from axiom MA5 and considered adding an MA6.

(1) Recap of the F-version. The essential algebraic axioms for (F, ≤, ≪) are collected on p. 23. Implication diagrams among the interpolation/(in)compatibility-disjunction axioms are on p. 22 and pp. 45-46. Those pages work relative to a given subset A ⊂ M, which leads directly to notions of 'A-spacious' and 'A-polyhedral' objects. In the absolute 'spacious' and 'polyhedral' cases he will now spell out the axioms At C(spac) and At C(pol).

(2) Shift of viewpoint. He passes to a structure (M, ≤, ≪, ≬). Here M is a set of 'multistrata' (elements, not subsets), and ≬ is a reflexive, symmetric 'compatibility' relation. The data look slightly more complicated, though he has removed the family F̃ ⊂ P(M) as explicit data. The axiom system nevertheless seems simpler, at least for some packets of axioms.

(3) Proposal. Treat (M, ≤, ≪, ≬) as an independent algebraic structure, tentatively called a 'pré-chiffon', 'algèbre de multistrates' or 'algèbre de strates'. Such a structure can underlie several chiffons, each realized as a family F ⊂ P(M). Continuing on p. 51, there is a largest one: the family of all subsets of M that are closed under ≤ and pairwise compatible for ≬.

Page 51 then lists the 'Prat' axioms. They include:
- ≤ implies ≪;
- transitivity of the interior relation;
- existence of a ≤-minimal Y' ≤ Y with X interior to Y';
- ≬ reflexive, symmetric, and inherited by ≤-smaller elements;
- uniqueness of minimal objects among compatible ones;
- for compatible X, Y, X ≪ Y implies X ≤ Y.
A variant uses a dense subset Λ ⊂ M.

The problem this page serves is to find a finitary, purely combinatorial presentation of 'tame' spaces via their posets of strata. The topology is then recovered as a family of 'consistent' down-closed subsets.
